<node TEXT="Data gathering instruments" FOLDED="true" ID="ID_869979840" CREATED="1714585283839" MODIFIED="1714585295024">
<node TEXT="Interviews" ID="ID_1829010809" CREATED="1714585300567" MODIFIED="1714585304003">
<node TEXT="usefulness of interviews: investigating non-observable phenomena (e.g. participants&apos; perceptions or attitudes)" ID="ID_1475891582" CREATED="1714584199160" MODIFIED="1714584199160" LINK="project://16AA1F3483D6GEWV5A1JJLVG6C22R89VFR4E/../../../articulos/pedagogy/Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch.pdf">
<attribute NAME="key" VALUE="Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch"/>
<attribute NAME="title" VALUE="Second Language Research"/>
<attribute NAME="authors" VALUE="Mackey, Alison and Gass, Susan M."/>
<pdf_annotation type="HIGHLIGHTED_TEXT" page="190" object_id="3659022201527024336" object_number="1483"/>
</node>
<node TEXT="possible problems with interviews: may involve distortions of the facts by the interviewee through selective recall, memory loss, or &quot;halo effect&quot; (picking up cues from the researcher regarding what they think the researcher wants them to say), as well as by the researcher (subjectivity in recording and interpreting data). Also possibility for cross-cultural pragmatic failure (inappropriate questions, different connotation of terms, etc.). Suggestions to overcome these: include open-ended discussion as well as direct questions (researcher keeps silent or includes an &quot;anything else?&quot; question rather than accepting the first answer), trying to make the interviewee as comfortable as possible (begin with small talk to relax them, using their L1 if preferred), place key questions in the middle (interviewee may be nervous at the beginning and tired or rushed at the end), mirror responses by repeating them neutrally and providing an opportunity for further input." ID="ID_330058163" CREATED="1714584199182" MODIFIED="1714584199182" LINK="project://16AA1F3483D6GEWV5A1JJLVG6C22R89VFR4E/../../../articulos/pedagogy/Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch.pdf">
<attribute NAME="key" VALUE="Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch"/>
<attribute NAME="title" VALUE="Second Language Research"/>
<attribute NAME="authors" VALUE="Mackey, Alison and Gass, Susan M."/>
<pdf_annotation type="HIGHLIGHTED_TEXT" page="191" object_id="6068941460121938575" object_number="1522"/>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Journals/Diaries" ID="ID_826766919" CREATED="1714585364511" MODIFIED="1714585373268">
<node TEXT="advantages of journal/diary data: it can yield insights into aspects that the researcher had not considered and therefore would not specifically prompt for (in an interview or survey, for example); even when some structure is provided (e.g. topics for the diary), they will access the phenomena from a process other than the researchers&apos;" ID="ID_681760716" CREATED="1714584199196" MODIFIED="1714584199196" LINK="project://16AA1F3483D6GEWV5A1JJLVG6C22R89VFR4E/../../../articulos/pedagogy/Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch.pdf">
<attribute NAME="key" VALUE="Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch"/>
<attribute NAME="title" VALUE="Second Language Research"/>
<attribute NAME="authors" VALUE="Mackey, Alison and Gass, Susan M."/>
<pdf_annotation type="HIGHLIGHTED_TEXT" page="195" object_id="9048208939479563933" object_number="1595"/>
</node>
<node TEXT="growing practice of researchers using diaries as data to provide some structure by including prompts relating to specific issues, guidance questions, range and amount of writing per entry" ID="ID_1788837607" CREATED="1714584199171" MODIFIED="1714584199171" LINK="project://16AA1F3483D6GEWV5A1JJLVG6C22R89VFR4E/../../../articulos/pedagogy/Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch.pdf">
