<node TEXT="Coding" ID="ID_1170833011" CREATED="1714585630579" MODIFIED="1714585633070">
<node TEXT="Open coding: in qualitative research, coding schemes emerge from the data (initial categories are established after a first pass through the data) rather than being preimposed prior to coding" ID="ID_1674125918" CREATED="1714584199244" MODIFIED="1714584199244" LINK="project://16AA1F3483D6GEWV5A1JJLVG6C22R89VFR4E/../../../articulos/pedagogy/Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch.pdf">
<attribute NAME="key" VALUE="Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch"/>
<attribute NAME="title" VALUE="Second Language Research"/>
<attribute NAME="authors" VALUE="Mackey, Alison and Gass, Susan M."/>
<pdf_annotation type="HIGHLIGHTED_TEXT" page="258" object_id="4380124485371069224" object_number="1831"/>
</node>
<node TEXT="basic example of coding qualitative data: labels, questions, overviews, headings, etc. are assigned to chunks or data; they are not yet precise in the early stages, the data rather than the theory drives the coding. Themes and topics emerge from the first round, they are structured in the process, and a conceptual schema or organization system eventually emerges." ID="ID_1512467890" CREATED="1714584199217" MODIFIED="1714584199217" LINK="project://16AA1F3483D6GEWV5A1JJLVG6C22R89VFR4E/../../../articulos/pedagogy/Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch.pdf">
<attribute NAME="key" VALUE="Mackey2005SecondLanguageResearch"/>
<attribute NAME="title" VALUE="Second Language Research"/>
<attribute NAME="authors" VALUE="Mackey, Alison and Gass, Susan M."/>
<pdf_annotation type="HIGHLIGHTED_TEXT" page="258" object_id="8590127771297040033" object_number="1840"/>
</node>
<node TEXT="Procedures" ID="ID_1724551170" CREATED="1714585982264" MODIFIED="1714585985636">
<node TEXT="coding data, first stage: identifying extracts of significance and generating initial codes based on the researcher&apos;s interpretation of the data in an inductive way. &quot;_Codes_ are building blocks for identifying patterns of meaning in the data, underpinned by a central organizing concept&quot; (Clarke &amp; Braun, 2017)" ID="ID_128014652" CREATED="1714584199067" MODIFIED="1714584199067" LINK="project://16AA1F3483D6GEWV5A1JJLVG6C22R89VFR4E/../../../articulos/pedagogy/Peel2020BeginnersGuideApplied.pdf">
<attribute NAME="key" VALUE="Peel2020BeginnersGuideApplied"/>
<attribute NAME="title" VALUE="A Beginner&apos;s Guide to Applied Educational Research using Thematic Analysis"/>
<attribute NAME="authors" VALUE="Peel, Karen L."/>
<pdf_annotation type="HIGHLIGHTED_TEXT" page="9" object_id="4580853003432214567" object_number="183"/>
</node>
<node TEXT="Themes and thematic analysis" ID="ID_1194691164" CREATED="1714586627715" MODIFIED="1714586635456" MOVED="1714586666769">
<node TEXT="Thematic analysis: more involvement from the researcher, who is tasked with identifying and describing implicit and explicit ideas in the data (themes); codes are developed to represent the themes and applied to other text chunks for further analysis. Reliability is a greater concern compared to word-based analysis, as there is more interpretation" ID="ID_1798914157" CREATED="1714584199629" MODIFIED="1714584199629" LINK="project://16AA1F3483D6GEWV5A1JJLVG6C22R89VFR4E/../../../articulos/pedagogy/Guest2012AppliedThematicAnalysis.pdf">
<attribute NAME="key" VALUE="Guest2012AppliedThematicAnalysis"/>
<attribute NAME="title" VALUE="Applied Thematic Analysis"/>
<attribute NAME="authors" VALUE="Guest, Greg and MacQueen, Kathleen and Namey, Emily"/>
<pdf_annotation type="HIGHLIGHTED_TEXT" page="11" object_id="69523297446821501" object_number="2434"/>
</node>
<node TEXT="two main categories of themes: structural (imposed by the research design) and emergent (what is observed in the context of the research design)" ID="ID_742099619" CREATED="1714584199969" MODIFIED="1714584199969" LINK="project://16AA1F3483D6GEWV5A1JJLVG6C22R89VFR4E/../../../articulos/pedagogy/Guest2012AppliedThematicAnalysis.pdf">
<attribute NAME="key" VALUE="Guest2012AppliedThematicAnalysis"/>
